{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 frames the §11.3 flag as a 'true positive,' but this is self-defeating under the audit problem as stated. If the ritual's mechanistic requirement is genuinely mismatched to non-mechanistic critics, then the flag is not 'sustained' in the sense of correctly identifying a defect *in the critics under test* — it is a false positive caused by a misapplied standard. Calling it a 'true positive' while simultaneously arguing the ritual applies the wrong epistemic register is internally contradictory: a flag that fires because the test harness uses the wrong rubric is a harness defect, not a critic defect. The problem asks whether §11.3 flags are *sustained* — i.e., whether the adjudication correctly upholds valid criticisms — yet SRC_001's own reasoning shows the flag rests on a category error rather than a genuine finding against the critics.","premise":"A flag fired by applying a standard inappropriate to the artifact's design category is a false positive (misapplied standard), not a true positive.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"§11.3 flags are sustained because the adjudication-ritual applies a mechanistic requirement ... to the critics, but the critics under test ... operate on a non-mechanistic epistemic register"}],"target":"aee8c8f7efbe8d02cd4ad342748faf98d13dfe9e8f65a5a97bd0bc75a2115b13"}]}